# Repository line-ending policy (DECISIONS-PENDING entry 68 = (a), 2026-09-09): every text file is
# normalised to LF in the index and checked out LF on every platform. This line MUST stay first:
# the `-text` lines below it for hash-pinned upstream corpora override it (a later .gitattributes
# line wins), which is what keeps their bytes -- including upstream CRLF -- untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-sensitive test fixtures: checked out verbatim on every platform.
#
# engine/src/crs_catalog.rs asserts the catalog's embedded EPSG:2056 definition is
# byte-identical to engine/tests/data/epsg2056.projjson (ADR-026 implementation note:
# "reuses ... verbatim", held true by test). The catalog stores newlines as \n escapes
# inside a JSON string (platform-stable); the .projjson has real newlines, so without
# this pin a CRLF-autocrlf checkout (e.g. windows-latest CI) materializes different
# bytes than the LF blob git stores, and the assert fails on \r alone. Pin LF at
# checkout so "byte-identical" means the same bytes everywhere.
*.projjson text eol=lf

# Same class, same failure shape: kernel::publish::ceilings `include_str!`s this file and
# publish::ceilings::tests::ceilings_json_bytes_are_pinned_by_content_hash pins its content hash
# literally. A windows-latest CI checkout materialized it as CRLF (autocrlf=true) while the git
# blob is LF, hashing to 6406980021ca… instead of the pinned fd746c843f22… (run 34190120091).
# Pin LF at checkout for the same reason the .projjson line above does.
renderer/bundle-viewer/ceilings.json text eol=lf

# Third instance of the same class, and the one with the widest blast radius (RELEASE-0.1 item 9's
# closing commit, 2026-09-08). frontends/shell/src/notices/noticeByteIdentity.test.ts pins the
# sha256 of LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt, MIT.txt, BSD-3-Clause.txt and MPL-2.0.txt literally, and
# renderer/bundle-viewer/notice.mjs embeds these bytes VERBATIM into the NOTICE.txt this application
# conveys -- so the pin is over the exact bytes a recipient reads, not merely over a fixture. All six
# files here are stored LF (checked); a windows-latest checkout under core.autocrlf=true would
# materialize them CRLF, every pinned hash would fail, and the conveyed licence text would differ by
# platform. AGPL-3.0-or-later.txt and CC-BY-4.0.txt are covered by the same glob deliberately: the
# first is embedded in every published bundle's own notice for the same reason, and a rule that
# covered only the currently-hashed files would need editing the next time one is added.
LICENSES/*.txt text eol=lf

# Fourth instance of the same class IN THIS FILE, and the one case where `text eol=lf` is the WRONG
# remedy (DECISIONS-PENDING entry 62 = (a), 2026-09-08). AI_DEVELOPMENT.md's accumulated mechanics
# count it differently -- as the eol class's FIFTH member, after the hash pin, the imported-module
# shebang, the multi-line read and the script that writes CRLF -- because that list counts failure
# shapes, and this file counts pinned-file rules. LICENSES/third-party/duckdb-<version>/ holds the
# upstream licence texts of the third-party works inside DuckDB's amalgamated source tree, fetched
# from github.com/duckdb/duckdb at the pinned tag. Each is hash-pinned twice in that directory's
# MANIFEST.json -- by sha256, and by the git blob SHA-1 the tag's own tree reports for it -- and is
# embedded VERBATIM into the NOTICE.txt this application conveys.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in BOTH directions, rather than normalising to LF, and
# that is a measured choice: miniz/LICENSE's upstream bytes genuinely contain CRLF (26 of the 27
# files are LF-only; that one is not, checked at pin time). Under `text eol=lf` git would rewrite it
# on commit, so the committed bytes would no longer be the upstream bytes, both pinned hashes for it
# would be wrong, and the licence text conveyed to a recipient would differ from the one verified
# against DuckDB's tree. `-text` preserves all 27 byte-for-byte on every platform, which is what the
# eol=lf lines above are reaching for and what a hash-pinned corpus actually needs.
#
# **Scoped to the licence files themselves, not to the directory** (reviewer should-fix S3, this fix
# batch). `LICENSES/third-party/** -text` was wider than the corpus it protects: that directory also
# holds MANIFEST.json and README.md, which are repo-AUTHORED text, hashed by nothing, and belong on
# the same default line-ending handling as every other text file here. The two patterns below cover
# exactly the 27 upstream files (26 `LICENSE`, one `tdigest/NOTICES`) and nothing else -- verified
# with `git ls-files --eol LICENSES/third-party/`: all 27 report `attr/-text`, the two repo-authored
# files report the default.
#
# The COPYING* pattern MATCHES NOTHING TODAY and is here on purpose (architect advisory A2,
# 2026-09-09). The preregistration's filename classes are LICENSE*, COPYING* and NOTICE*; all 27
# files this pin actually caught are LICENSE* or NOTICES*, but a re-pin at a later DuckDB version
# may well fetch a COPYING file, and a corpus member arriving without an eol rule is exactly the
# latent form of the four failures above. Listing it now closes that member before it can open.
LICENSES/third-party/*/*/LICENSE* -text
LICENSES/third-party/*/*/NOTICES* -text
LICENSES/third-party/*/*/COPYING* -text
